A||N||6||MR2KLR||How do I Loosen the Alt. belt on my 88 Coupe?||||1||
Z||000000||MR2KLR||12-03-2001||05:07 PM||||<BR>I can't do it? what's wrong with me? I got the top bolt on the alternator loose (It looks like the adj. bolt) and it dose nothing. my haynes manul just says to loosen the adj. bolt, remove belt & replace. They don't have a pic or and better directions. Somebody help.<P>Thanks,<BR>Tom<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||12-03-2001||05:10 PM|||| <A HREF="http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/serpent.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/serpent.htm</A> <P>I think thats what your looking for. Its from Ogre's Cave at the bottom of the page. I had to replace a belt on my 88 coupe, it was easy. Just make sure you use a quality belt.||||reg||14
Z||000002||avengador1||12-03-2001||07:31 PM||||There are two bolts that have to be loosened. The top bolt is the pivot bolt and the lower is where the adjustment to the belt tension is made. The lower bolt is a little trickier to get to. Don't forget to tighten the bottom one first after replacing the belt and putting the right tension on the belt, and don't forget to retighten the top one when your done. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-03-2001||07:44 PM||||You don’t have to tighten the belts on 87/88 coupes. It has an automatic tensioning serpent belt, not Vbelt like other fieros. They are much superior to the V belts. I hate V belts, they belong on tractors not cars lol.<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ronnie||12-03-2001||11:12 PM||||Master, you do have to tighten the belts on 87-88 coupes too. They look like a serpentine belt , but do not have a tensioner.||||reg||6
Z||000005||Galen||12-03-2001||11:27 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> I've replaced enough belts on my POS.. eerrr 88 to know about this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> there is a tensioner on em... <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">  <BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT></B> - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/index.shtml" TARGET=_blank>Fierolinks</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||12-04-2001||12:01 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ronnie:<BR><B>Master, you do have to tighten the belts on 87-88 coupes too. They look like a serpentine belt , but do not have a tensioner.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>On the 87 & 88 coupes with the tech 4 engine the belt has no manual adjustment. It's just one continuous belt with an automatic belt tensioner. No adjustment can be made manually. If the belt is squeaky, it's time for a new one. Recommend replacement annually. The belts on the V-6 is the same as the rest of the model years and require periodic adjustments. Hope this helps.<P>To answer the question, place a 15 mm wrench on the tensioner pully bolt head and rotate it to the front of the car from the top to loosen the belt. Installation is done the same way, but be prepared it will need to move farther than the removal process.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
